WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.640
As with every James Gunn movie, observant fans&nbsp;
will notice plenty of extra goodies and Easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.880
eggs hiding in plain sight, and his latest&nbsp;
superhero adventure is no exception. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.880
are several of the more subtle references and&nbsp;
Easter eggs that you missed in The Suicide Squad.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.920
Moments before the battle on Jotunheim, Task Force&nbsp;
X briefs the Thinker on the myriad ways he needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.080
to behave himself, lest they dispatch him with&nbsp;
extreme prejudice. During this rooftop scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.600
Harley Quinn repeatedly interjects with&nbsp;
jokey cautions about things like covering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.000
his mouth whenever he coughs. The Easter egg,&nbsp;
however, is buried in one of these lines:&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.600
"If we find out you have personalized&nbsp;
license plates, you die."&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.040
Vanity plates can often be annoying, so&nbsp;
the line gets a laugh whether you read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.480
something deeper into it or not. But&nbsp;
Harley's hatred of vehicle signage is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.480
actually a reference to her ex-boyfriend,&nbsp;
none other than the Joker. It's also one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.800
the only times in The Suicide Squad where they&nbsp;
acknowledge the existence of the earlier film.&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.640
In the previous outing for the titular team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.880
the Joker was infamously played by Jared Leto.&nbsp;
Much like everything else with the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.320
the initial response to his look was&nbsp;
massive, promising internet buzz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.753
but the final product has subsequently been&nbsp;
deemed a disappointment.

00:01:01.954 --> 00:01:06.400
 Symbolic of the&nbsp;Leto Joker's on-the-nose tendencies that&nbsp;
made him hard to swallow was his vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.120
an adapted Infiniti G35 dubbed "The Vaydor."
That vehicle also featured vanity plates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.440
read "HAHAHA." Harley has since moved on in&nbsp;
the relationship department, but clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.240
she still has some things that annoy&nbsp;
her when it comes to her ex-puddin'.

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:23.200
When the team assembles in a charming local&nbsp;
Corto Maltese watering hole for a few drinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.080
the club contains several notable faces. One of&nbsp;
the dancers is none other than Pom Klementieff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.960
the distinctive-looking Canadian-born French actor&nbsp;
and model who played Mantis in 2017's Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.600
the Galaxy Vol. 2 as well as Avengers: Infinity&nbsp;
War and Avengers: Endgame. She doesn't have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.680
lines this time around, but sharp-eyed James&nbsp;
Gunn fans know that he has a tight, fun-loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.734
group of friends and collaborators. It's fun&nbsp;
to see her offering support for the filmmaker.&nbsp;

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:50.160
You can also spot the legendary Lloyd Kaufman.&nbsp;
He’s the mastermind behind the Troma Entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.080
indie film factory. And he has given rise&nbsp;
to multiple film talents including Gunn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.234 --> 00:01:58.800
who in 1995 got his start writing Tromeo and&nbsp;
Juliet. It was at Troma that Gunn learned how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.181
to write and produce films, scout locations,&nbsp;
work with actors, and make and distribute ads for the finished product. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.181 --> 00:02:08.400
Gunn also worked on such&nbsp;
other Troma productions as 1999's Terror Firmer.&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.400
Kaufman can be spotted on the dance floor,&nbsp;
clinging to a young woman while dancing alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.640
Peacemaker. To be a fly on the wall for whatever&nbsp;
conversation took place between those two!&nbsp;

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:19.360
Gunn also pays tribute to his&nbsp;
former mentor Kaufman with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.000
blink-and-you'll-miss-it clip from Troma's&nbsp;
most famous film, the 1984 cult classic The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.760
Toxic Avenger. Next time you watch The&nbsp;
Suicide Squad, see if you can spot it.

00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:32.419
A pair of end credits scenes bring&nbsp;
back two members of the Suicide Squad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:35.360
These two characters could not be any&nbsp;
more different, never met in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.172
and would seem to have very different&nbsp;
futures in the DC Extended Universe.&nbsp;

00:02:39.172 --> 00:02:43.440
First, we see the bizarre, bug-eyed Weasel&nbsp;
resurrected. Played on set by James' brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.600
Sean Gunn, who also provided reference points&nbsp;
for Rocket Raccoon in the Guardians of the Galaxy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:54.000
films, the character first appeared in the pages&nbsp;
of DC Comics via 1985's The Fury of Firestorm #36.&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.680
Believe it or not, the John Monroe version&nbsp;
of Weasel was a Stanford University student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.240
and then a teacher at Vandemeer University. Much&nbsp;
like Polka-Dot Man, his presence in comics over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.560
the years has generally been as a punchline,&nbsp;
both on the page, and for fans in real life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.040
In those early comics, he was a serial killer&nbsp;
whose appearance came from a sharp-clawed costume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.174
In the comics, he slashed the throat of the&nbsp;
Thinker, causing Rick Flag to kill him.

00:03:15.174 --> 00:03:19.859
 As far&nbsp;as the movie is concerned, Weasel is seen coughing&nbsp;
up water and surviving the carnage on the beach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.859 --> 00:03:24.400
He scampers away, perhaps headed into town to grab&nbsp;
a drink with Lloyd Kaufman and Pom Klementieff.&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.400
In the second end credits scene, we see a pair&nbsp;
of Waller's newly emboldened minions rushing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.480
the hospital, shocked that someone survived the&nbsp;
battle of Jotunheim. You can be forgiven if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.332
have your fingers crossed for Polka-Dot Man, but&nbsp;
alas, lying in that hospital bed is Peacemaker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.332 --> 00:03:41.920
This revelation almost certainly foreshadows the&nbsp;
character's resurrection in a Gunn-engineered HBO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.720
Max Peacemaker series, one the filmmaker recently&nbsp;
announced he had completed 8 episodes of filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.000
alongside Cena. Peacemaker is currently slated to&nbsp;
debut on the streaming service in January 2022.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.061
Talk about a line that gets maximum&nbsp;
impact with minimum dialogue.

00:03:56.061 --> 00:03:58.955
 When&nbsp;Amanda Waller introduces Robert DuBois by saying:&nbsp;

00:03:58.955 --> 00:04:02.960
"He's in prison for putting Superman&nbsp;
in the UCU with a kryptonite bullet."&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:07.120
You immediately know he is not a man to be&nbsp;
trifled with. Alas, there is no Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.200
cameo in The Suicide Squad, but there is a&nbsp;
precedent to the whole kryptonite bullet idea.&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.880
Ask anyone who knows anything about the man from&nbsp;
Krypton, and they can tell you the one way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.840
kill Superman: Kryptonite. The fictional material&nbsp;
first appeared in the radio serial The Adventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:27.217
of Superman in June of 1943, followed by its first&nbsp;
comic book appearance in November 1949's Superman&nbsp;#61. 

00:04:27.217 --> 00:04:31.280
The material originates from the hero's&nbsp;
home planet, is generally harmless to humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.760
but could potentially prove lethal for the Man of&nbsp;
Steel. Both Batman and Lex Luthor are generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.680
known to keep some close, just in case.
The character of Bloodsport dates back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.200
to 1987's Superman vol. 2 #4 and has since&nbsp;
become a longtime adversary of Superman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.600
Initially depicted in that post-Rambo time period&nbsp;
as a Vietnam veteran, he was brought into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.400
fold by Luthor, who recruited him to assassinate&nbsp;
Superman after equipping him with a Punisher-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.880
stash of weapons that included a gun that fired&nbsp;
needles filled with kryptonite. This Bloodsport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.560
was angry at the citizens of Metropolis,&nbsp;
saying they took for granted the freedoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.880
he and his brother had fought hard to preserve.&nbsp;
After Bloodsport slaughtered dozens of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.440
Superman intervened, taking a Kryptonite bullet&nbsp;
in the process, and requiring medical attention.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.800
Take a close look at the poster for The Suicide&nbsp;
Squad, and you'll notice several Easter eggs.&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.160
One is the raining starfish,&nbsp;
an obvious reference to Starro.&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.640
It's the big bad, mind-controlling monster&nbsp;
Task Force X battles at the end of the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.320
Another seems to be referring to the beloved&nbsp;
DC Comics miniseries that introduced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.834
modern Suicide Squad and contained&nbsp;
some of their earliest misadventures.&nbsp;

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:35.360
First spotted by the site Comics Beat,&nbsp;
the reference can be found masquerading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.880
as a palm tree frond just under the&nbsp;
word "The" in the poster tagline:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.920
"They're dying to save the world." Look super&nbsp;
close, and you'll see it: The word "Legends."&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.000
Most likely, this Easter egg refers to the&nbsp;
warmly remembered, six-issue Legends comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.680
series DC released in 1986 and 1987. The comics&nbsp;
featured new takes on the Flash, Justice League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.040
and Captain Marvel, but perhaps most&nbsp;
significantly introduced the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.480
incarnation of the Suicide Squad in issue #3.
The series had DC heroes battling Darkseid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.120
and the third issue had Amanda Waller organizing&nbsp;
a Black Ops team to take on Glorious Godfrey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.600
one of Darkseid's elite and a New God&nbsp;
of Apokolips. She selected Rick Flag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.720
Bronze Tiger, Deadshot, Enchantress, and Captain&nbsp;
Boomerang for the mission. Many of whom are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.800
featured in Gunn and David Ayer’s films.
Comics fans love to see a tip of the cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.880
to those who came before, and this minor&nbsp;
acknowledgment of John Ostrander, Len Wein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:29.760
John Byrne, Karl Kesel, and others who helped&nbsp;
build the modern Suicide Squad is a nice touch.&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.040
Gunn is undoubtedly one of the most&nbsp;
comic book-savvy super fans working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.880
in Hollywood today, and this seems to be an&nbsp;
acknowledgment that the publishing of Legends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.663
which would have come out when he was 20&nbsp;
years old, was a formative experience.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.400
His demise might just get the&nbsp;
biggest laugh in The Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.720
an impressive feat in a movie filled with&nbsp;
punchlines as potent as Bloodsport's weaponry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.080
Emerging quietly in the background of&nbsp;
the story, a seemingly insignificant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.920
van-driving character goes from chauffeur&nbsp;
to tag-along to full-fledged martyr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.560
inspiring the Squad to take down Jotunheim and&nbsp;
ultimately rebel against Waller and her program.&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.160
His name is Milton, and he first gained the&nbsp;
attention of many sharp-eyed moviegoers when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.477
he could be glimpsed in the background of the&nbsp;
rooftop scene, strutting around like he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.477 --> 00:07:12.917
another super-powered member of the Suicide Squad.&nbsp;
When a fan posted a screengrab and asked "Who is&nbsp;this dude?????" 

00:07:12.917 --> 00:07:16.400
the Twitter-savvy filmmaker&nbsp;
simply replied: "Oh, that? That's Milton."&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.080
Comic fans immediately leapt into action,&nbsp;
speculating that he could be the sideshow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.960
character Milton Fine, also known as the Amazing&nbsp;
Brainiac. Or perhaps he’s a gender-swapped version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.640
of the female Amazon warrior Hippolyta&nbsp;
Milton. If true, that could lead to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.800
connection with Wonder Woman, which could mean&nbsp;
a Justice League and Suicide Squad crossover!&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.000
Nope, it’s just Milton. The stone-faced, ill-fated&nbsp;
driver is played by Julio Ruiz, an actor who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:41.840
has appeared on Vice Principals, Preacher, and&nbsp;
S.W.A.T. If you want to see something adorable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.880
track down a March 2021 article from Ruiz's&nbsp;
hometown newspaper, the Laredo Morning Times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.800
It proudly hypes up the fact that he's&nbsp;
appearing alongside Margot Robbie, Idris Elba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.160
and other Hollywood stars in a summer tentpole&nbsp;
superhero film, but clearly has no idea why.&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.160
Milton's barely acknowledged death triggers&nbsp;
Harley Quinn, as does the acknowledgment from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.080
the others that they hardly realized he wasn't&nbsp;
waiting in the van. Like the death of Javelin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.134
it emboldens her to turn tragedy into&nbsp;
triumph and avenge her fallen comrades.&nbsp;

00:08:08.134 --> 00:08:11.762
Are there any grander implications&nbsp;
to this storyline in the DCEU?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.762 --> 00:08:16.240
Can we look forward to a Batman/Milton team-up&nbsp;
movie in the near future? Probably not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.720
but you can follow Julio Ruiz on social media&nbsp;
for more information on the mysterious Milton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.518
and where his story goes from here.

